Fix the SGI MC's DMA page-table base mask, which truncates every DMA mapping
whose page table lives above physical 0x0fffffff -- i.e. every one in the upper
half of a 256 MB Indy.

THE DEFECT THIS FIXES

  PANIC: bad istack sp:8835afa8

IRIX 6.5 on `indy_4610` died with that panic -- byte-identical stack pointer
across every sighting -- when a visitor opened Toolchest -> Help -> "Welcome to
SGI" (which launches Netscape). It killed the live exhibit and it needed a
manual restart. Reproduced 8 times out of 9 on clones of the shipped golden.

ROOT CAUSE

`sgi_mc_device::dma_translate()` walks a guest-supplied page table to translate
a GIO/DMA address. The two address fields it decodes are encoded identically --
bits 6..25, scaled by 4096 -- but they are masked differently:

	const uint32_t pte = m_space->read_dword(((m_dma_tlb_entry_lo[entry] & 0x003fffc0) << 6) + (vpn_lo << 2));
	                                                                       ^^^^^^^^^^ 16 bits
	return ((pte & 0x03ffffc0) << 6) + offset;
	              ^^^^^^^^^^ 20 bits

`0x003fffc0 << 6` spans bits 12..27, so the page-table base saturates at
0x0fffffff. IP24 main memory starts at physical 0x0800'0000, so:

- with <= 128 MB installed, RAM ends at 0x0fff'ffff and the narrow mask is a
  no-op -- every address it can produce is correct;
- with 256 MB installed (RAM 0x0800'0000-0x17ff'ffff), any page table the guest
  places in the upper half is read from the WRONG physical address. Instrumented
  on a live boot, IRIX put one at **0x17de7000** (entry_lo 0x005f79c2) and MAME
  read it from **0x07de7000** -- below the start of RAM, i.e. unmapped. The
  resulting garbage PTEs send the DMA engine's reads and writes to arbitrary
  physical addresses, scribbling on kernel memory until something structural
  breaks. The same corruption also shows up mildly as a garbled Netscape window
  on a run that happens not to panic.

That is the whole RAM-size dependence, and it is measured:

| RAM (our SIMMS default) | result |
|---|---|
| 256 MB (banks A+B 4x32M) — what the tile shipped | **PANIC 8/9** |
| 192 MB (A 4x32M + B 4x16M) | no panic |
| 128 MB (A 4x32M, B empty) | no panic (3/3) |
| 128 MB (A+B 4x16M) | no panic |
| 64 MB, 16 MB (stock MAME) | no panic |

so it is OUR `mame-indy-256mb-ram.patch` that exposed a latent upstream bug --
one nobody upstream would meet, because stock MAME ships this machine with
16 MB. The bug is present in MAME 0.288 and unchanged in 0.289 and on master.

Ruled out along the way, each by experiment rather than by argument: the DRC
code-cache size, the Newport/shm/ds1386 patches (the pre-shm binary
`sgi.prev-a33944d3` panics identically), `MIPS3DRC_STRICT_VERIFY` and
`MIPS3DRC_COMPATIBLE_OPTIONS`, and a genuine but unrelated MIPS3 DRC bug (the
software-interrupt check in `generate_update_cycles()` omits the `Status.IE` /
`EXL` / `ERL` guards that the interpreter and the DRC's own full-interrupt check
both apply -- adding them changes nothing here, and it is still worth reporting
upstream separately).

NOTE, not fixed here: `dma_translate()` returns physical **0** when no DMA TLB
entry matches, and physical 0 is install_ram'd as an alias of the first 512 KiB
of RAM -- i.e. the start of the kernel image. That fires during boot in every
configuration, including stock 16 MB, so it is neither this panic nor something
our RAM patch introduced; it is logged as a separate upstream concern.

diff --git a/src/mame/sgi/mc.cpp b/src/mame/sgi/mc.cpp
index 79186754..0d21af2a 100644
--- a/src/mame/sgi/mc.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/sgi/mc.cpp
@@ -210,7 +210,13 @@ uint32_t sgi_mc_device::dma_translate(uint32_t address)
 		if ((address & 0xffc00000) == (m_dma_tlb_entry_hi[entry] & 0xffc00000))
 		{
 			const uint32_t vpn_lo = (address & 0x003ff000) >> 12;
-			const uint32_t pte = m_space->read_dword(((m_dma_tlb_entry_lo[entry] & 0x003fffc0) << 6) + (vpn_lo << 2));
+			// The page-table base field is encoded exactly like the PTE field
+			// below (bits 6..25, scaled by 4096), so it must use the SAME mask.
+			// 0x003fffc0 drops bits 22..25 and truncates any page table above
+			// physical 0x0fffffff -- i.e. every one in the upper half of a
+			// 256 MB machine, which then reads below the start of RAM.
+			const uint32_t ptbase = (m_dma_tlb_entry_lo[entry] & 0x03ffffc0) << 6;
+			const uint32_t pte = m_space->read_dword(ptbase + (vpn_lo << 2));
 			const uint32_t offset = address & 0xfff;
 			return ((pte & 0x03ffffc0) << 6) + offset;
 		}
